  "answer_text": "Despite his name, **Flounder is not actually a flounder**. \n\nReal-life flounders are flat, brown, camouflage-colored bottom-dwellers with both eyes on one side of their head. Disney's Flounder, however, is a chubby, brightly colored tropical fish. \n\nHis actual species varies depending on which version of *The Little Mermaid* you are looking at:\n\n### 1. The Original Animated Film (1989)\nIn the animated classic, Flounder is a generic, stylized **tropical reef fish**. \n* **The Visual Inspiration:** Animators modeled him after colorful reef fish. Marine experts generally agree that his round body, yellow-and-blue coloring, and fin shape most closely resemble a **blue angelfish** (*Holacanthus bermudensis*). \n* **The In-Universe Joke:** Throughout the animated franchise (specifically in the prequel television series), Ariel frequently teases him by calling him a **\"guppy\"** when he gets scared, leading some in-universe sources to classify him as one.\n\n### 2. The Live-Action Remake (2023)\nFor the live-action adaptation, the filmmakers wanted him to look like a realistic fish that naturally has his signature yellow-and-blue striped color scheme. In this version, Flounder is officially a **sergeant major fish** (a type of damselfish native to the Atlantic Ocean).",
